Wood is a widely utilized construction material, prized for its renewability, abundance, and excellent strength-to-weight ratio. However, its structural applications are often limited by comparatively low values for absolute structural properties, a sensitivity to moisture not found in many other building materials, and unequal strength per direction (known as anisotropy). This paper investigates a scalable chemical-mechanical hybrid treatment, aiming to improve on those aforementioned absolute properties. This methodology, derived from previous research done in the field, involves a two-step approach: the wood is first boiled in an alkaline-sulfite solution to partially delignify the wood, reducing the rigidity of the cell wall matrix. Following delignification and neutralization via citric acid, the samples undergo thermo-mechanical densification (hot pressing) at various pressures, increasing material density.

The results obtained over the course of this experiment indicate substantial improvement of mechanical properties in comparison to ordinary pine wood, with some slight caveats. Despite a maximum compression of #qty(7, "MPa"), many quantities (such as density) peaked at #qty(6, "MPa") instead. Importantly, the derived quantities of the modulus of rupture (MOR) and strength-to-weight ratio (specific strength) showed clear positive trends with increasing pressure. Flexural strength values improved substantially in a similar manner. Charpy impact testing indicated that a moderate (5 to 6 hour) dissolution had no noticeable effect on the impact energy, suggesting that the brittleness of the material is not worsened by the densification process; an extended 8 hour dissolution, however, reduced the impact energy sharply.

Ultimately, this study supports the idea that a targeted chemical pre-treatment, when used in conjunction with hot pressing, could be used to bulk-transform ordinary wood into a versatile, high-performance structural material. As such, this approach maximizes the usage of the desirable properties of wood by offering further benefits in sustainability, efficiency, and renewability.